{
  "dom_id": "step-q50108",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/fb373420ddaa81aa41e85145dce11d71068740e8039522c5d68d861de606bec3.png)\n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by\n${y}^{\u2032}=(x-a)(y-b).$\nFind the value of $a+b.$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/e9011b88061abae826e12742ccd47a272bef9e969f204cd7e8ca63edf908a7f8.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=2$ and $y=2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=(x-2)(y-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=2,$ $b=2,$ and $a+b=4.$",
